Reason (R): A rectangle is a parallelogram with all angles equal to 90°.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Mathematics', 'Quadrilaterals', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'In a parallelogram with equal diagonals, adjacent angles are supplementary and congruent, hence each equals 90°, forming a rectangle.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000015', 'Express 0.2353535... (i.e. 0.235 with bar on 35) in the p/q form, where p and q are integers and q ≠ 0.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Number Systems', 2, 'Let x = 0.2353535... Multiplying by 10: 10x = 2.353535... (Eq 1). Multiplying by 1000: 1000x = 235.353535... (Eq 2). Subtracting Eq 1 from Eq 2: 990x = 233 -> x = 233 / 990.', '1 Mark for setting up equations with appropriate multipliers 10 and 1000; 1 Mark for subtraction and final simplified fraction 233/990', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000016', 'Find the value of the polynomial p(y) = 2 + y + 2y² - y³ at: (i) y = 0, (ii) y = 2.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 2, '(i) p(0) = 2 + 0 + 2(0)² - (0)³ = 2. (ii) p(2) = 2 + 2 + 2(2)² - (2)³ = 4 + 8 - 8 = 4.', '1 Mark for calculating p(0) = 2; 1 Mark for calculating p(2) = 4', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000017', 'In which quadrants or on which axes do the following points lie: A(0, -4), B(-2, 3), C(5, 0), D(4, -3)?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Coordinate Geometry', 2, 'A(0, -4) lies on the negative y-axis. B(-2, 3) lies in Quadrant II. C(5, 0) lies on the positive x-axis. D(4, -3) lies in Quadrant IV.', '0.5 Mark for each correctly identified quadrant/axis location (total 2 Marks)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000018', 'If a point C lies between two points A and B such that AC = BC, then prove that AC = (1/2)AB. Explain by drawing the figure.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Introduction to Euclid''s Geometry', 2, 'Given: AC = BC. Adding AC to both sides: AC + AC = BC + AC -> 2AC = BC + AC. Since BC + AC coincides with line segment AB (Euclid Axiom 4: Things which coincide with one another are equal to one another): 2AC = AB -> AC = (1/2)AB.', '1 Mark for geometric representation & adding equal segment; 1 Mark for invoking Euclid''s axiom to conclude AC = 1/2 AB', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000019', 'Find the curved surface area and total surface area of a hemisphere of radius 21 cm. (Use π = 22/7)', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Surface Areas and Volumes', 2, 'Radius r = 21 cm. 1. Curved Surface Area (CSA) = 2πr² = 2 * (22/7) * 21 * 21 = 2 * 22 * 3 * 21 = 2,772 cm². 2. Total Surface Area (TSA) = 3πr² = 3 * (22/7) * 21 * 21 = 3 * 22 * 3 * 21 = 4,158 cm².', '1 Mark for correct CSA formula & result (2,772 cm²); 1 Mark for correct TSA formula & result (4,158 cm²)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-00000000001a', 'Rationalise the denominator of: 1 / (7 + 3√2).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Number Systems', 3, 'Multiplying numerator and denominator by conjugate (7 - 3√2): [1 * (7 - 3√2)] / [(7 + 3√2)(7 - 3√2)] = (7 - 3√2) / [7² - (3√2)²] = (7 - 3√2) / [49 - (9 * 2)] = (7 - 3√2) / (49 - 18) = (7 - 3√2) / 31.', '1 Mark for identifying conjugate; 1 Mark for denominator difference of squares (49 - 18); 1 Mark for final rationalized fraction', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-00000000001b', 'Factorise completely using the identity: 27x³ + y³ + z³ - 9xyz.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 3, 'Rewrite as: (3x)³ + y³ + z³ - 3(3x)(y)(z). Using identity a³ + b³ + c³ - 3abc = (a + b + c)(a² + b² + c² - ab - bc - ca) where a = 3x, b = y, c = z: = (3x + y + z)[(3x)² + y² + z² - (3x)(y) - y(z) - z(3x)] = (3x + y + z)(9x² + y² + z² - 3xy - yz - 3zx).', '1 Mark for recognizing cubic identity; 1 Mark for substitution of terms; 1 Mark for expanded second polynomial factor', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c', 'Draw the graph of the linear equation 2x + y = 6. Find the coordinates of the points where the line crosses the coordinate axes.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Linear Equations in Two Variables', 3, 'Find solutions: When x = 0, y = 6 -> (0, 6). When y = 0, 2x = 6 -> x = 3 -> (3, 0). When x = 1, y = 4 -> (1, 4). Plotting these points on graph and drawing a line through them gives the graph. The line crosses the x-axis at (3, 0) and the y-axis at (0, 6).', '1 Mark for finding table of three points; 1 Mark for plotting straight line graph; 1 Mark for identifying intercepts (3, 0) and (0, 6)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-00000000001d', 'In lines and angles, prove that if two lines intersect each other, then the vertically opposite angles are equal.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Lines and Angles', 3, 'Let two lines AB and CD intersect at point O. Ray OA stands on line CD -> ∠AOC + ∠AOD = 180° (Linear pair, Eq 1). Ray OD stands on line AB -> ∠AOD + ∠BOD = 180° (Linear pair, Eq 2). From Eq 1 and Eq 2: ∠AOC + ∠AOD = ∠AOD + ∠BOD. Subtracting ∠AOD from both sides: ∠AOC = ∠BOD. Similarly, it can be proven that ∠AOD = ∠BOC.', '1 Mark for diagram and linear pair identification; 1 Mark for equating two linear pair equations; 1 Mark for concluding equality of opposite angles', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-00000000001e', 'Find the area of a triangle whose sides are 13 cm, 14 cm, and 15 cm using Heron''s formula.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Heron''s Formula', 3, 'Sides a = 13, b = 14, c = 15. Semi-perimeter s = (a + b + c) / 2 = (13 + 14 + 15) / 2 = 42 / 2 = 21 cm. Factors: (s - a) = 21 - 13 = 8, (s - b) = 21 - 14 = 7, (s - c) = 21 - 15 = 6. Area = √[s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)] = √[21 * 8 * 7 * 6] = √[(3 * 7) * (2 * 2 * 2) * 7 * (2 * 3)] = √[7² * 3² * 2⁴] = 7 * 3 * 4 = 84 cm².', '1 Mark for semi-perimeter s = 21 cm; 1 Mark for substituting into Heron''s formula; 1 Mark for prime factor square root evaluation to 84 cm²', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f', 'Prove that equal chords of a circle subtend equal angles at the center.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Circles', 3, 'Given: A circle with center O in which chord AB = chord CD. To prove: ∠AOB = ∠COD. Proof: In ΔAOB and ΔCOD: OA = OC (Radii of the same circle), OB = OD (Radii of the same circle), AB = CD (Given). By SSS congruence criterion: ΔAOB ≅ ΔCOD. Hence, ∠AOB = ∠COD (CPCTC - Corresponding parts of congruent triangles).', '1 Mark for figure, given, and to-prove; 1 Mark for SSS congruence setup with radii; 1 Mark for CPCTC angle equality deduction', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000020', 'Prove that the sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°. Hence, in ΔPQR, if bisectors of ∠Q and ∠R meet at point O, prove that ∠QOR = 90° + (1/2)∠P.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Lines and Angles', 5, '(Part 1): Given ΔABC. Draw line XY through A parallel to BC. ∠XAB + ∠BAC + ∠YAC = 180° (Straight line). Since XY || BC, ∠XAB = ∠B and ∠YAC = ∠C (Alternate interior angles). Substituting: ∠B + ∠A + ∠C = 180°. (Part 2): In ΔOQR: ∠QOR + (1/2)∠Q + (1/2)∠R = 180° -> ∠QOR = 180° - (1/2)(∠Q + ∠R). From ΔPQR: ∠Q + ∠R = 180° - ∠P. Substituting: ∠QOR = 180° - (1/2)(180° - ∠P) = 180° - 90° + (1/2)∠P = 90° + (1/2)∠P.', '2.5 Marks for construction & proof of angle sum property (180°); 2.5 Marks for algebraic angle bisector proof yielding 90° + 1/2 ∠P', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000021', '(a) Factorise the cubic polynomial: p(x) = x³ - 23x² + 142x - 120. (b) Verify by using factor theorem and polynomial long division.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 5, 'Constant term is -120. Factors of -120 include ±1, ±2, etc. Testing x = 1: p(1) = 1³ - 23(1)² + 142(1) - 120 = 1 - 23 + 142 - 120 = 143 - 143 = 0. Therefore, (x - 1) is a factor. Dividing p(x) by (x - 1): x³ - 23x² + 142x - 120 = (x - 1)(x² - 22x + 120). Splitting the middle term of quotient: x² - 22x + 120 = x² - 12x - 10x + 120 = x(x - 12) - 10(x - 12) = (x - 12)(x - 10). Complete factorisation: p(x) = (x - 1)(x - 10)(x - 12).', '1.5 Marks for finding initial root x=1 via factor theorem; 2 Marks for long division to get quadratic x² - 22x + 120; 1.5 Marks for splitting middle term into (x - 10)(x - 12)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000022', 'A dome of a building is in the form of a hemisphere. From inside, it was whitewashed at the cost of ₹ 498.96. If the cost of whitewashing is ₹ 2.00 per square meter, find: (i) the inside surface area of the dome, (ii) the volume of the air inside the dome. (Use π = 22/7)', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Surface Areas and Volumes', 5, '(i) Cost = Rate * Area -> Inside surface area = Total Cost / Rate = 498.96 / 2 = 249.48 m². (ii) For hemisphere, inside CSA = 2πr² = 249.48. 2 * (22/7) * r² = 249.48 -> r² = (249.48 * 7) / 44 = 1746.36 / 44 = 39.69 -> r = √39.69 = 6.3 meters. Volume of air inside dome = (2/3)πr³ = (2/3) * (22/7) * (6.3)³ = (2/3) * (22/7) * 250.047 = (44/21) * 250.047 = 523.908 m³.', '2 Marks for finding inside surface area 249.48 m²; 1.5 Marks for calculating radius r = 6.3 m; 1.5 Marks for calculating volume 523.908 m³', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000023', 'The length, breadth, and height of a room are 8 m 25 cm, 6 m 75 cm, and 4 m 50 cm respectively. Determine the longest tape which can measure the three dimensions of the room exactly.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Number Systems', 5, 'Convert all dimensions into centimeters: Length l = 825 cm, Breadth b = 675 cm, Height h = 450 cm. The length of the longest tape is given by the Highest Common Factor (HCF) of 825, 675, and 450. Prime factorisations: 825 = 3 * 5² * 11. 675 = 3³ * 5². 450 = 2 * 3² * 5². HCF(825, 675, 450) = 3¹ * 5² = 3 * 25 = 75 cm. Hence, the longest measuring tape required is 75 cm (or 0.75 m).', '1.5 Marks for unit conversion to cm; 2 Marks for prime factorisation of numbers; 1.5 Marks for finding HCF = 75 cm', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000024', 'Answer the following using Heron''s formula: (i) Find the semi-perimeter ''s'' of the triangular roof. (ii) Calculate the total surface area of the triangular roof. (iii) Calculate the total cost incurred in coating the entire roof.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Mathematics', 'Heron''s Formula', 4, '(i) s = (50 + 78 + 112) / 2 = 240 / 2 = 120 m. (ii) (s - a) = 120 - 50 = 70; (s - b) = 120 - 78 = 42; (s - c) = 120 - 112 = 8. Area = √[120 * 70 * 42 * 8] = √[(10 * 12) * (10 * 7) * (6 * 7) * 8] = √[10² * 7² * (12 * 48)] = √[10² * 7² * 576] = 10 * 7 * 24 = 1,680 m². (iii) Total cost = Area * Rate = 1680 * 40 = ₹ 67,200.', '1 Mark for semi-perimeter 120 m; 2 Marks for Heron area computation 1,680 m²; 1 Mark for total cost ₹ 67,200', 'To promote green energy, a school designs a triangular solar panel installation on its auditorium roof. The three sides of the triangular roof measure 50 m, 78 m, and 112 m. The school management plans to coat the roof with reflective solar sealant costing ₹ 40 per square meter before installing the panels.', NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000025', 'Based on the Cartesian layout, answer: (i) In which quadrant does Hurdle A lie, and what is its perpendicular distance from the x-axis? (ii) What geometric shape is formed by joining the points A, B, C, and D in order? (iii) Find the perimeter of the figure ABCD.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Mathematics', 'Coordinate Geometry', 4, '(i) Hurdle A(4, 3) lies in Quadrant I. Its perpendicular distance from the x-axis is equal to the absolute ordinate |y| = 3 units. (ii) AB = 3 - (-3) = 6 units (vertical). BC = 4 - (-4) = 8 units (horizontal). CD = 6 units. DA = 8 units. All interior angles are 90°. Hence, ABCD forms a Rectangle. (iii) Perimeter = 2 * (length + breadth) = 2 * (8 + 6) = 2 * 14 = 28 units.', '1 Mark for Quadrant I and distance 3; 1.5 Marks for proving Rectangle; 1.5 Marks for calculating perimeter 28 units', 'Students of Class 9 organized a sports day race. The track is marked on a Cartesian grid where the starting point is at origin O(0, 0). Hurdle A is placed at point (4, 3), water booth B is at (4, -3), medical station C is at (-4, -3), and finish flag D is at (-4, 3).', NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020007-0000-4000-8000-000000000026', 'Answer the following: (i) Calculate the slant height ''l'' of the conical tent. (ii) Find the area of canvas required to erect the tent (excluding the floor). (iii) Find the total cost of canvas fabric required.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Mathematics', 'Surface Areas and Volumes', 4, '(i) Slant height l = √(r² + h²) = √(7² + 24²) = √(49 + 576) = √625 = 25 meters. (ii) Canvas required = Curved Surface Area = πrl = (22/7) * 7 * 25 = 22 * 25 = 550 m². (iii) Total cost = Area * Rate = 550 * 70 = ₹ 38,500.', '1 Mark for slant height l = 25 m; 2 Marks for curved surface area 550 m²; 1 Mark for total cost ₹ 38,500', 'A residential welfare society constructed a conical canvas tent to serve as a community health camp. The radius of the base of the conical tent is 7 m, and its vertical height is 24 m. The canvas fabric used costs ₹ 70 per square meter.', NULL, 'MEDIUM', true)
        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

        -- Questions for Test 8
Reason (R): The highest power of variable x in a quadratic polynomial is 2.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'p(x) has maximum exponent 2, making its degree 2, which satisfies the definition of a quadratic polynomial.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000014', 'Assertion (A): If the diagonals of a parallelogram are equal, then it is a rectangle.
Reason (R): A rectangle is a parallelogram with all angles equal to 90°.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Mathematics', 'Quadrilaterals', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'In a parallelogram with equal diagonals, adjacent angles are supplementary and congruent, hence each equals 90°, forming a rectangle.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000015', 'Express 0.2353535... (i.e. 0.235 with bar on 35) in the p/q form, where p and q are integers and q ≠ 0.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Number Systems', 2, 'Let x = 0.2353535... Multiplying by 10: 10x = 2.353535... (Eq 1). Multiplying by 1000: 1000x = 235.353535... (Eq 2). Subtracting Eq 1 from Eq 2: 990x = 233 -> x = 233 / 990.', '1 Mark for setting up equations with appropriate multipliers 10 and 1000; 1 Mark for subtraction and final simplified fraction 233/990', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000016', 'Find the value of the polynomial p(y) = 2 + y + 2y² - y³ at: (i) y = 0, (ii) y = 2.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 2, '(i) p(0) = 2 + 0 + 2(0)² - (0)³ = 2. (ii) p(2) = 2 + 2 + 2(2)² - (2)³ = 4 + 8 - 8 = 4.', '1 Mark for calculating p(0) = 2; 1 Mark for calculating p(2) = 4', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000017', 'In which quadrants or on which axes do the following points lie: A(0, -4), B(-2, 3), C(5, 0), D(4, -3)?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Coordinate Geometry', 2, 'A(0, -4) lies on the negative y-axis. B(-2, 3) lies in Quadrant II. C(5, 0) lies on the positive x-axis. D(4, -3) lies in Quadrant IV.', '0.5 Mark for each correctly identified quadrant/axis location (total 2 Marks)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000018', 'If a point C lies between two points A and B such that AC = BC, then prove that AC = (1/2)AB. Explain by drawing the figure.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Introduction to Euclid''s Geometry', 2, 'Given: AC = BC. Adding AC to both sides: AC + AC = BC + AC -> 2AC = BC + AC. Since BC + AC coincides with line segment AB (Euclid Axiom 4: Things which coincide with one another are equal to one another): 2AC = AB -> AC = (1/2)AB.', '1 Mark for geometric representation & adding equal segment; 1 Mark for invoking Euclid''s axiom to conclude AC = 1/2 AB', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000019', 'Find the curved surface area and total surface area of a hemisphere of radius 21 cm. (Use π = 22/7)', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Surface Areas and Volumes', 2, 'Radius r = 21 cm. 1. Curved Surface Area (CSA) = 2πr² = 2 * (22/7) * 21 * 21 = 2 * 22 * 3 * 21 = 2,772 cm². 2. Total Surface Area (TSA) = 3πr² = 3 * (22/7) * 21 * 21 = 3 * 22 * 3 * 21 = 4,158 cm².', '1 Mark for correct CSA formula & result (2,772 cm²); 1 Mark for correct TSA formula & result (4,158 cm²)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-00000000001a', 'Rationalise the denominator of: 1 / (7 + 3√2).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Number Systems', 3, 'Multiplying numerator and denominator by conjugate (7 - 3√2): [1 * (7 - 3√2)] / [(7 + 3√2)(7 - 3√2)] = (7 - 3√2) / [7² - (3√2)²] = (7 - 3√2) / [49 - (9 * 2)] = (7 - 3√2) / (49 - 18) = (7 - 3√2) / 31.', '1 Mark for identifying conjugate; 1 Mark for denominator difference of squares (49 - 18); 1 Mark for final rationalized fraction', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-00000000001b', 'Factorise completely using the identity: 27x³ + y³ + z³ - 9xyz.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 3, 'Rewrite as: (3x)³ + y³ + z³ - 3(3x)(y)(z). Using identity a³ + b³ + c³ - 3abc = (a + b + c)(a² + b² + c² - ab - bc - ca) where a = 3x, b = y, c = z: = (3x + y + z)[(3x)² + y² + z² - (3x)(y) - y(z) - z(3x)] = (3x + y + z)(9x² + y² + z² - 3xy - yz - 3zx).', '1 Mark for recognizing cubic identity; 1 Mark for substitution of terms; 1 Mark for expanded second polynomial factor', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c', 'Draw the graph of the linear equation 2x + y = 6. Find the coordinates of the points where the line crosses the coordinate axes.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Linear Equations in Two Variables', 3, 'Find solutions: When x = 0, y = 6 -> (0, 6). When y = 0, 2x = 6 -> x = 3 -> (3, 0). When x = 1, y = 4 -> (1, 4). Plotting these points on graph and drawing a line through them gives the graph. The line crosses the x-axis at (3, 0) and the y-axis at (0, 6).', '1 Mark for finding table of three points; 1 Mark for plotting straight line graph; 1 Mark for identifying intercepts (3, 0) and (0, 6)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-00000000001d', 'In lines and angles, prove that if two lines intersect each other, then the vertically opposite angles are equal.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Lines and Angles', 3, 'Let two lines AB and CD intersect at point O. Ray OA stands on line CD -> ∠AOC + ∠AOD = 180° (Linear pair, Eq 1). Ray OD stands on line AB -> ∠AOD + ∠BOD = 180° (Linear pair, Eq 2). From Eq 1 and Eq 2: ∠AOC + ∠AOD = ∠AOD + ∠BOD. Subtracting ∠AOD from both sides: ∠AOC = ∠BOD. Similarly, it can be proven that ∠AOD = ∠BOC.', '1 Mark for diagram and linear pair identification; 1 Mark for equating two linear pair equations; 1 Mark for concluding equality of opposite angles', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-00000000001e', 'Find the area of a triangle whose sides are 13 cm, 14 cm, and 15 cm using Heron''s formula.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Heron''s Formula', 3, 'Sides a = 13, b = 14, c = 15. Semi-perimeter s = (a + b + c) / 2 = (13 + 14 + 15) / 2 = 42 / 2 = 21 cm. Factors: (s - a) = 21 - 13 = 8, (s - b) = 21 - 14 = 7, (s - c) = 21 - 15 = 6. Area = √[s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)] = √[21 * 8 * 7 * 6] = √[(3 * 7) * (2 * 2 * 2) * 7 * (2 * 3)] = √[7² * 3² * 2⁴] = 7 * 3 * 4 = 84 cm².', '1 Mark for semi-perimeter s = 21 cm; 1 Mark for substituting into Heron''s formula; 1 Mark for prime factor square root evaluation to 84 cm²', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f', 'Prove that equal chords of a circle subtend equal angles at the center.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Circles', 3, 'Given: A circle with center O in which chord AB = chord CD. To prove: ∠AOB = ∠COD. Proof: In ΔAOB and ΔCOD: OA = OC (Radii of the same circle), OB = OD (Radii of the same circle), AB = CD (Given). By SSS congruence criterion: ΔAOB ≅ ΔCOD. Hence, ∠AOB = ∠COD (CPCTC - Corresponding parts of congruent triangles).', '1 Mark for figure, given, and to-prove; 1 Mark for SSS congruence setup with radii; 1 Mark for CPCTC angle equality deduction', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000020', 'Prove that the sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°. Hence, in ΔPQR, if bisectors of ∠Q and ∠R meet at point O, prove that ∠QOR = 90° + (1/2)∠P.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Lines and Angles', 5, '(Part 1): Given ΔABC. Draw line XY through A parallel to BC. ∠XAB + ∠BAC + ∠YAC = 180° (Straight line). Since XY || BC, ∠XAB = ∠B and ∠YAC = ∠C (Alternate interior angles). Substituting: ∠B + ∠A + ∠C = 180°. (Part 2): In ΔOQR: ∠QOR + (1/2)∠Q + (1/2)∠R = 180° -> ∠QOR = 180° - (1/2)(∠Q + ∠R). From ΔPQR: ∠Q + ∠R = 180° - ∠P. Substituting: ∠QOR = 180° - (1/2)(180° - ∠P) = 180° - 90° + (1/2)∠P = 90° + (1/2)∠P.', '2.5 Marks for construction & proof of angle sum property (180°); 2.5 Marks for algebraic angle bisector proof yielding 90° + 1/2 ∠P', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000021', '(a) Factorise the cubic polynomial: p(x) = x³ - 23x² + 142x - 120. (b) Verify by using factor theorem and polynomial long division.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 5, 'Constant term is -120. Factors of -120 include ±1, ±2, etc. Testing x = 1: p(1) = 1³ - 23(1)² + 142(1) - 120 = 1 - 23 + 142 - 120 = 143 - 143 = 0. Therefore, (x - 1) is a factor. Dividing p(x) by (x - 1): x³ - 23x² + 142x - 120 = (x - 1)(x² - 22x + 120). Splitting the middle term of quotient: x² - 22x + 120 = x² - 12x - 10x + 120 = x(x - 12) - 10(x - 12) = (x - 12)(x - 10). Complete factorisation: p(x) = (x - 1)(x - 10)(x - 12).', '1.5 Marks for finding initial root x=1 via factor theorem; 2 Marks for long division to get quadratic x² - 22x + 120; 1.5 Marks for splitting middle term into (x - 10)(x - 12)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000022', 'A dome of a building is in the form of a hemisphere. From inside, it was whitewashed at the cost of ₹ 498.96. If the cost of whitewashing is ₹ 2.00 per square meter, find: (i) the inside surface area of the dome, (ii) the volume of the air inside the dome. (Use π = 22/7)', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Surface Areas and Volumes', 5, '(i) Cost = Rate * Area -> Inside surface area = Total Cost / Rate = 498.96 / 2 = 249.48 m². (ii) For hemisphere, inside CSA = 2πr² = 249.48. 2 * (22/7) * r² = 249.48 -> r² = (249.48 * 7) / 44 = 1746.36 / 44 = 39.69 -> r = √39.69 = 6.3 meters. Volume of air inside dome = (2/3)πr³ = (2/3) * (22/7) * (6.3)³ = (2/3) * (22/7) * 250.047 = (44/21) * 250.047 = 523.908 m³.', '2 Marks for finding inside surface area 249.48 m²; 1.5 Marks for calculating radius r = 6.3 m; 1.5 Marks for calculating volume 523.908 m³', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000023', 'The length, breadth, and height of a room are 8 m 25 cm, 6 m 75 cm, and 4 m 50 cm respectively. Determine the longest tape which can measure the three dimensions of the room exactly.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Number Systems', 5, 'Convert all dimensions into centimeters: Length l = 825 cm, Breadth b = 675 cm, Height h = 450 cm. The length of the longest tape is given by the Highest Common Factor (HCF) of 825, 675, and 450. Prime factorisations: 825 = 3 * 5² * 11. 675 = 3³ * 5². 450 = 2 * 3² * 5². HCF(825, 675, 450) = 3¹ * 5² = 3 * 25 = 75 cm. Hence, the longest measuring tape required is 75 cm (or 0.75 m).', '1.5 Marks for unit conversion to cm; 2 Marks for prime factorisation of numbers; 1.5 Marks for finding HCF = 75 cm', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000024', 'Answer the following using Heron''s formula: (i) Find the semi-perimeter ''s'' of the triangular roof. (ii) Calculate the total surface area of the triangular roof. (iii) Calculate the total cost incurred in coating the entire roof.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Mathematics', 'Heron''s Formula', 4, '(i) s = (50 + 78 + 112) / 2 = 240 / 2 = 120 m. (ii) (s - a) = 120 - 50 = 70; (s - b) = 120 - 78 = 42; (s - c) = 120 - 112 = 8. Area = √[120 * 70 * 42 * 8] = √[(10 * 12) * (10 * 7) * (6 * 7) * 8] = √[10² * 7² * (12 * 48)] = √[10² * 7² * 576] = 10 * 7 * 24 = 1,680 m². (iii) Total cost = Area * Rate = 1680 * 40 = ₹ 67,200.', '1 Mark for semi-perimeter 120 m; 2 Marks for Heron area computation 1,680 m²; 1 Mark for total cost ₹ 67,200', 'To promote green energy, a school designs a triangular solar panel installation on its auditorium roof. The three sides of the triangular roof measure 50 m, 78 m, and 112 m. The school management plans to coat the roof with reflective solar sealant costing ₹ 40 per square meter before installing the panels.', NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000025', 'Based on the Cartesian layout, answer: (i) In which quadrant does Hurdle A lie, and what is its perpendicular distance from the x-axis? (ii) What geometric shape is formed by joining the points A, B, C, and D in order? (iii) Find the perimeter of the figure ABCD.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Mathematics', 'Coordinate Geometry', 4, '(i) Hurdle A(4, 3) lies in Quadrant I. Its perpendicular distance from the x-axis is equal to the absolute ordinate |y| = 3 units. (ii) AB = 3 - (-3) = 6 units (vertical). BC = 4 - (-4) = 8 units (horizontal). CD = 6 units. DA = 8 units. All interior angles are 90°. Hence, ABCD forms a Rectangle. (iii) Perimeter = 2 * (length + breadth) = 2 * (8 + 6) = 2 * 14 = 28 units.', '1 Mark for Quadrant I and distance 3; 1.5 Marks for proving Rectangle; 1.5 Marks for calculating perimeter 28 units', 'Students of Class 9 organized a sports day race. The track is marked on a Cartesian grid where the starting point is at origin O(0, 0). Hurdle A is placed at point (4, 3), water booth B is at (4, -3), medical station C is at (-4, -3), and finish flag D is at (-4, 3).', NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020008-0000-4000-8000-000000000026', 'Answer the following: (i) Calculate the slant height ''l'' of the conical tent. (ii) Find the area of canvas required to erect the tent (excluding the floor). (iii) Find the total cost of canvas fabric required.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Mathematics', 'Surface Areas and Volumes', 4, '(i) Slant height l = √(r² + h²) = √(7² + 24²) = √(49 + 576) = √625 = 25 meters. (ii) Canvas required = Curved Surface Area = πrl = (22/7) * 7 * 25 = 22 * 25 = 550 m². (iii) Total cost = Area * Rate = 550 * 70 = ₹ 38,500.', '1 Mark for slant height l = 25 m; 2 Marks for curved surface area 550 m²; 1 Mark for total cost ₹ 38,500', 'A residential welfare society constructed a conical canvas tent to serve as a community health camp. The radius of the base of the conical tent is 7 m, and its vertical height is 24 m. The canvas fabric used costs ₹ 70 per square meter.', NULL, 'MEDIUM', true)
        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

        -- Questions for Test 9
Reason (R): The highest power of variable x in a quadratic polynomial is 2.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'p(x) has maximum exponent 2, making its degree 2, which satisfies the definition of a quadratic polynomial.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000014', 'Assertion (A): If the diagonals of a parallelogram are equal, then it is a rectangle.
Reason (R): A rectangle is a parallelogram with all angles equal to 90°.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Mathematics', 'Quadrilaterals', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'In a parallelogram with equal diagonals, adjacent angles are supplementary and congruent, hence each equals 90°, forming a rectangle.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000015', 'Express 0.2353535... (i.e. 0.235 with bar on 35) in the p/q form, where p and q are integers and q ≠ 0.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Number Systems', 2, 'Let x = 0.2353535... Multiplying by 10: 10x = 2.353535... (Eq 1). Multiplying by 1000: 1000x = 235.353535... (Eq 2). Subtracting Eq 1 from Eq 2: 990x = 233 -> x = 233 / 990.', '1 Mark for setting up equations with appropriate multipliers 10 and 1000; 1 Mark for subtraction and final simplified fraction 233/990', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000016', 'Find the value of the polynomial p(y) = 2 + y + 2y² - y³ at: (i) y = 0, (ii) y = 2.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 2, '(i) p(0) = 2 + 0 + 2(0)² - (0)³ = 2. (ii) p(2) = 2 + 2 + 2(2)² - (2)³ = 4 + 8 - 8 = 4.', '1 Mark for calculating p(0) = 2; 1 Mark for calculating p(2) = 4', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000017', 'In which quadrants or on which axes do the following points lie: A(0, -4), B(-2, 3), C(5, 0), D(4, -3)?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Coordinate Geometry', 2, 'A(0, -4) lies on the negative y-axis. B(-2, 3) lies in Quadrant II. C(5, 0) lies on the positive x-axis. D(4, -3) lies in Quadrant IV.', '0.5 Mark for each correctly identified quadrant/axis location (total 2 Marks)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000018', 'If a point C lies between two points A and B such that AC = BC, then prove that AC = (1/2)AB. Explain by drawing the figure.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Introduction to Euclid''s Geometry', 2, 'Given: AC = BC. Adding AC to both sides: AC + AC = BC + AC -> 2AC = BC + AC. Since BC + AC coincides with line segment AB (Euclid Axiom 4: Things which coincide with one another are equal to one another): 2AC = AB -> AC = (1/2)AB.', '1 Mark for geometric representation & adding equal segment; 1 Mark for invoking Euclid''s axiom to conclude AC = 1/2 AB', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000019', 'Find the curved surface area and total surface area of a hemisphere of radius 21 cm. (Use π = 22/7)', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Mathematics', 'Surface Areas and Volumes', 2, 'Radius r = 21 cm. 1. Curved Surface Area (CSA) = 2πr² = 2 * (22/7) * 21 * 21 = 2 * 22 * 3 * 21 = 2,772 cm². 2. Total Surface Area (TSA) = 3πr² = 3 * (22/7) * 21 * 21 = 3 * 22 * 3 * 21 = 4,158 cm².', '1 Mark for correct CSA formula & result (2,772 cm²); 1 Mark for correct TSA formula & result (4,158 cm²)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-00000000001a', 'Rationalise the denominator of: 1 / (7 + 3√2).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Number Systems', 3, 'Multiplying numerator and denominator by conjugate (7 - 3√2): [1 * (7 - 3√2)] / [(7 + 3√2)(7 - 3√2)] = (7 - 3√2) / [7² - (3√2)²] = (7 - 3√2) / [49 - (9 * 2)] = (7 - 3√2) / (49 - 18) = (7 - 3√2) / 31.', '1 Mark for identifying conjugate; 1 Mark for denominator difference of squares (49 - 18); 1 Mark for final rationalized fraction', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-00000000001b', 'Factorise completely using the identity: 27x³ + y³ + z³ - 9xyz.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 3, 'Rewrite as: (3x)³ + y³ + z³ - 3(3x)(y)(z). Using identity a³ + b³ + c³ - 3abc = (a + b + c)(a² + b² + c² - ab - bc - ca) where a = 3x, b = y, c = z: = (3x + y + z)[(3x)² + y² + z² - (3x)(y) - y(z) - z(3x)] = (3x + y + z)(9x² + y² + z² - 3xy - yz - 3zx).', '1 Mark for recognizing cubic identity; 1 Mark for substitution of terms; 1 Mark for expanded second polynomial factor', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c', 'Draw the graph of the linear equation 2x + y = 6. Find the coordinates of the points where the line crosses the coordinate axes.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Linear Equations in Two Variables', 3, 'Find solutions: When x = 0, y = 6 -> (0, 6). When y = 0, 2x = 6 -> x = 3 -> (3, 0). When x = 1, y = 4 -> (1, 4). Plotting these points on graph and drawing a line through them gives the graph. The line crosses the x-axis at (3, 0) and the y-axis at (0, 6).', '1 Mark for finding table of three points; 1 Mark for plotting straight line graph; 1 Mark for identifying intercepts (3, 0) and (0, 6)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-00000000001d', 'In lines and angles, prove that if two lines intersect each other, then the vertically opposite angles are equal.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Lines and Angles', 3, 'Let two lines AB and CD intersect at point O. Ray OA stands on line CD -> ∠AOC + ∠AOD = 180° (Linear pair, Eq 1). Ray OD stands on line AB -> ∠AOD + ∠BOD = 180° (Linear pair, Eq 2). From Eq 1 and Eq 2: ∠AOC + ∠AOD = ∠AOD + ∠BOD. Subtracting ∠AOD from both sides: ∠AOC = ∠BOD. Similarly, it can be proven that ∠AOD = ∠BOC.', '1 Mark for diagram and linear pair identification; 1 Mark for equating two linear pair equations; 1 Mark for concluding equality of opposite angles', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-00000000001e', 'Find the area of a triangle whose sides are 13 cm, 14 cm, and 15 cm using Heron''s formula.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Heron''s Formula', 3, 'Sides a = 13, b = 14, c = 15. Semi-perimeter s = (a + b + c) / 2 = (13 + 14 + 15) / 2 = 42 / 2 = 21 cm. Factors: (s - a) = 21 - 13 = 8, (s - b) = 21 - 14 = 7, (s - c) = 21 - 15 = 6. Area = √[s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)] = √[21 * 8 * 7 * 6] = √[(3 * 7) * (2 * 2 * 2) * 7 * (2 * 3)] = √[7² * 3² * 2⁴] = 7 * 3 * 4 = 84 cm².', '1 Mark for semi-perimeter s = 21 cm; 1 Mark for substituting into Heron''s formula; 1 Mark for prime factor square root evaluation to 84 cm²', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f', 'Prove that equal chords of a circle subtend equal angles at the center.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Circles', 3, 'Given: A circle with center O in which chord AB = chord CD. To prove: ∠AOB = ∠COD. Proof: In ΔAOB and ΔCOD: OA = OC (Radii of the same circle), OB = OD (Radii of the same circle), AB = CD (Given). By SSS congruence criterion: ΔAOB ≅ ΔCOD. Hence, ∠AOB = ∠COD (CPCTC - Corresponding parts of congruent triangles).', '1 Mark for figure, given, and to-prove; 1 Mark for SSS congruence setup with radii; 1 Mark for CPCTC angle equality deduction', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000020', 'Prove that the sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°. Hence, in ΔPQR, if bisectors of ∠Q and ∠R meet at point O, prove that ∠QOR = 90° + (1/2)∠P.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Lines and Angles', 5, '(Part 1): Given ΔABC. Draw line XY through A parallel to BC. ∠XAB + ∠BAC + ∠YAC = 180° (Straight line). Since XY || BC, ∠XAB = ∠B and ∠YAC = ∠C (Alternate interior angles). Substituting: ∠B + ∠A + ∠C = 180°. (Part 2): In ΔOQR: ∠QOR + (1/2)∠Q + (1/2)∠R = 180° -> ∠QOR = 180° - (1/2)(∠Q + ∠R). From ΔPQR: ∠Q + ∠R = 180° - ∠P. Substituting: ∠QOR = 180° - (1/2)(180° - ∠P) = 180° - 90° + (1/2)∠P = 90° + (1/2)∠P.', '2.5 Marks for construction & proof of angle sum property (180°); 2.5 Marks for algebraic angle bisector proof yielding 90° + 1/2 ∠P', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000021', '(a) Factorise the cubic polynomial: p(x) = x³ - 23x² + 142x - 120. (b) Verify by using factor theorem and polynomial long division.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 5, 'Constant term is -120. Factors of -120 include ±1, ±2, etc. Testing x = 1: p(1) = 1³ - 23(1)² + 142(1) - 120 = 1 - 23 + 142 - 120 = 143 - 143 = 0. Therefore, (x - 1) is a factor. Dividing p(x) by (x - 1): x³ - 23x² + 142x - 120 = (x - 1)(x² - 22x + 120). Splitting the middle term of quotient: x² - 22x + 120 = x² - 12x - 10x + 120 = x(x - 12) - 10(x - 12) = (x - 12)(x - 10). Complete factorisation: p(x) = (x - 1)(x - 10)(x - 12).', '1.5 Marks for finding initial root x=1 via factor theorem; 2 Marks for long division to get quadratic x² - 22x + 120; 1.5 Marks for splitting middle term into (x - 10)(x - 12)', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000022', 'A dome of a building is in the form of a hemisphere. From inside, it was whitewashed at the cost of ₹ 498.96. If the cost of whitewashing is ₹ 2.00 per square meter, find: (i) the inside surface area of the dome, (ii) the volume of the air inside the dome. (Use π = 22/7)', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Surface Areas and Volumes', 5, '(i) Cost = Rate * Area -> Inside surface area = Total Cost / Rate = 498.96 / 2 = 249.48 m². (ii) For hemisphere, inside CSA = 2πr² = 249.48. 2 * (22/7) * r² = 249.48 -> r² = (249.48 * 7) / 44 = 1746.36 / 44 = 39.69 -> r = √39.69 = 6.3 meters. Volume of air inside dome = (2/3)πr³ = (2/3) * (22/7) * (6.3)³ = (2/3) * (22/7) * 250.047 = (44/21) * 250.047 = 523.908 m³.', '2 Marks for finding inside surface area 249.48 m²; 1.5 Marks for calculating radius r = 6.3 m; 1.5 Marks for calculating volume 523.908 m³', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000023', 'The length, breadth, and height of a room are 8 m 25 cm, 6 m 75 cm, and 4 m 50 cm respectively. Determine the longest tape which can measure the three dimensions of the room exactly.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Number Systems', 5, 'Convert all dimensions into centimeters: Length l = 825 cm, Breadth b = 675 cm, Height h = 450 cm. The length of the longest tape is given by the Highest Common Factor (HCF) of 825, 675, and 450. Prime factorisations: 825 = 3 * 5² * 11. 675 = 3³ * 5². 450 = 2 * 3² * 5². HCF(825, 675, 450) = 3¹ * 5² = 3 * 25 = 75 cm. Hence, the longest measuring tape required is 75 cm (or 0.75 m).', '1.5 Marks for unit conversion to cm; 2 Marks for prime factorisation of numbers; 1.5 Marks for finding HCF = 75 cm', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000024', 'Answer the following using Heron''s formula: (i) Find the semi-perimeter ''s'' of the triangular roof. (ii) Calculate the total surface area of the triangular roof. (iii) Calculate the total cost incurred in coating the entire roof.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Mathematics', 'Heron''s Formula', 4, '(i) s = (50 + 78 + 112) / 2 = 240 / 2 = 120 m. (ii) (s - a) = 120 - 50 = 70; (s - b) = 120 - 78 = 42; (s - c) = 120 - 112 = 8. Area = √[120 * 70 * 42 * 8] = √[(10 * 12) * (10 * 7) * (6 * 7) * 8] = √[10² * 7² * (12 * 48)] = √[10² * 7² * 576] = 10 * 7 * 24 = 1,680 m². (iii) Total cost = Area * Rate = 1680 * 40 = ₹ 67,200.', '1 Mark for semi-perimeter 120 m; 2 Marks for Heron area computation 1,680 m²; 1 Mark for total cost ₹ 67,200', 'To promote green energy, a school designs a triangular solar panel installation on its auditorium roof. The three sides of the triangular roof measure 50 m, 78 m, and 112 m. The school management plans to coat the roof with reflective solar sealant costing ₹ 40 per square meter before installing the panels.', NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000025', 'Based on the Cartesian layout, answer: (i) In which quadrant does Hurdle A lie, and what is its perpendicular distance from the x-axis? (ii) What geometric shape is formed by joining the points A, B, C, and D in order? (iii) Find the perimeter of the figure ABCD.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Mathematics', 'Coordinate Geometry', 4, '(i) Hurdle A(4, 3) lies in Quadrant I. Its perpendicular distance from the x-axis is equal to the absolute ordinate |y| = 3 units. (ii) AB = 3 - (-3) = 6 units (vertical). BC = 4 - (-4) = 8 units (horizontal). CD = 6 units. DA = 8 units. All interior angles are 90°. Hence, ABCD forms a Rectangle. (iii) Perimeter = 2 * (length + breadth) = 2 * (8 + 6) = 2 * 14 = 28 units.', '1 Mark for Quadrant I and distance 3; 1.5 Marks for proving Rectangle; 1.5 Marks for calculating perimeter 28 units', 'Students of Class 9 organized a sports day race. The track is marked on a Cartesian grid where the starting point is at origin O(0, 0). Hurdle A is placed at point (4, 3), water booth B is at (4, -3), medical station C is at (-4, -3), and finish flag D is at (-4, 3).', NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90020009-0000-4000-8000-000000000026', 'Answer the following: (i) Calculate the slant height ''l'' of the conical tent. (ii) Find the area of canvas required to erect the tent (excluding the floor). (iii) Find the total cost of canvas fabric required.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Mathematics', 'Surface Areas and Volumes', 4, '(i) Slant height l = √(r² + h²) = √(7² + 24²) = √(49 + 576) = √625 = 25 meters. (ii) Canvas required = Curved Surface Area = πrl = (22/7) * 7 * 25 = 22 * 25 = 550 m². (iii) Total cost = Area * Rate = 550 * 70 = ₹ 38,500.', '1 Mark for slant height l = 25 m; 2 Marks for curved surface area 550 m²; 1 Mark for total cost ₹ 38,500', 'A residential welfare society constructed a conical canvas tent to serve as a community health camp. The radius of the base of the conical tent is 7 m, and its vertical height is 24 m. The canvas fabric used costs ₹ 70 per square meter.', NULL, 'MEDIUM', true)
