        ('9002002b-0000-4000-8000-000000000009', 'Factorise completely using the identity: 27x³ + y³ + z³ - 9xyz.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 3, 'Rewrite as: (3x)³ + y³ + z³ - 3(3x)(y)(z). Using identity a³ + b³ + c³ - 3abc = (a + b + c)(a² + b² + c² - ab - bc - ca) where a = 3x, b = y, c = z: = (3x + y + z)[(3x)² + y² + z² - (3x)(y) - y(z) - z(3x)] = (3x + y + z)(9x² + y² + z² - 3xy - yz - 3zx).', '1 Mark for recognizing cubic identity; 1 Mark for substitution of terms; 1 Mark for expanded second polynomial factor', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('9002002b-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a', 'The length, breadth, and height of a room are 8 m 25 cm, 6 m 75 cm, and 4 m 50 cm respectively. Determine the longest tape which can measure the three dimensions of the room exactly.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Mathematics', 'Number Systems', 5, 'Convert all dimensions into centimeters: Length l = 825 cm, Breadth b = 675 cm, Height h = 450 cm. The length of the longest tape is given by the Highest Common Factor (HCF) of 825, 675, and 450. Prime factorisations: 825 = 3 * 5² * 11. 675 = 3³ * 5². 450 = 2 * 3² * 5². HCF(825, 675, 450) = 3¹ * 5² = 3 * 25 = 75 cm. Hence, the longest measuring tape required is 75 cm (or 0.75 m).', '1.5 Marks for unit conversion to cm; 2 Marks for prime factorisation of numbers; 1.5 Marks for finding HCF = 75 cm', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true)
